{"schema":2,"commit":"263637ed2cfa2348693992afa20ac0c65afba71f","branch":"fix/copilot-hook-and-crlf","base_sha":"d8b1d1902527bc936082aba3461a4107175e7650","message":"Record VS Code Copilot edits from the hook, and make Copilot hooks cheaper\n\nVS Code's agent mode sends camelCase tool input (filePath, oldString,\nnewString). The Copilot hook only read \"path\", so create_file and every\nother VS Code edit tool recorded nothing and the lines committed as Human.\nRead both shapes, add replace_string_in_file, and record\nmulti_replace_string_in_file and apply_patch per file. Edits from VS Code\nare gen_type chat, like the transcript watcher records them.\n\nThe transcript watcher now handles multi_replace_string_in_file too. With\nthe hook and the watchers all reporting the same edit, the daemon merges a\nsecond report from a different Copilot source (same file, same net lines,\nwithin 5 minutes) into the first row instead of storing it twice, which\ndouble-counted tokens and match budget.\n\nA tool call that names a file but carries no content is a read\n(read_file, list_dir, the CLI's view). It was recorded as an edit, and\ncaptureAuthorship then credited every uncommitted line in the file, the\nhuman's included, to Copilot. Reads are no longer recorded, and the\npre-hook skips them.\n\nRecording an edit spawned six git processes: the hook and the working-log\ncapture each resolved the repo, and HEAD content was read even when a\nbaseline existed. gitutil.Locate gets RepoID, top level and HEAD in one\ncall (falling back to the separate calls when that fails), the capture\nreuses it, and HEAD content is read only for a file's first edit. An edit\nnow costs two git processes, a pre-hook two, a read none. The pre-hook\nalso snapshots each file an apply_patch or multi_replace call names, which\npreviously fell back to HEAD.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e","coding_time_nanos":18769070300,"totals":{"added_lines":1056,"ai_added_lines":1